// The Common Cause is a civilian revolt against both armies, not a third conquering state.
export const REVOLT_CAST={
 'REV-DARIUS':{name:'Darius',origin:'Peasant organiser',role:'Common Cause speaker'},
 'REV-HUGH':{name:'Hugh atte Mill',origin:'Western mill villages',role:'Miller and food organiser'},
 'REV-ALICE':{name:'Alice atte Ford',origin:'Western river villages',role:'Wagonwright and relief driver'},
 'REV-MARYAM':{name:'Maryam bint Salim',origin:'Eastern irrigation villages',role:'Ditch keeper and healer'},
 'REV-FARID':{name:'Farid ibn Yunus',origin:'Eastern levy villages',role:'Former levy recruit'}
};
export const REVOLT_ART_IDS=Object.keys(REVOLT_CAST);
export const REVOLT_EVENTS=[
 {id:'civeton-levy',room:'town',character:'REV-HUGH',title:'The miller at the levy board',stage:1,at:[19,20],text:["They have taken two sacks for the column and left a prayer over the empty bin. I cannot grind a prayer into bread. My cousin across the border says their collectors do exactly the same.","You are looking for your friends? Then look at the carts as well as the banners. A road full of marching boys is a road somebody else must feed."]},
 {id:'mill-levy',room:'scrub',character:'REV-HUGH',title:'Two levies, one mill',stage:1,at:[18,17],text:["First Light took our flour. Dawn Bloom took the seed we hid for spring. Both receipts promise the same thing: payment when the war is won. We cannot eat a promise.","You carry a fighting partner, {player}. That makes you somebody an officer can use. If you want me to judge you differently, give me something other than another victory."],battle:'TRN-REV-HUGH'},
 {id:'first-light-writ',room:'dorelem',character:null,title:'A First Light denunciation',stage:2,at:[34,22],text:["By order of the march command: the speaker Darius denies divine authority, shelters deserters and incites the common levy to disobedience. He is declared a heretic. No household is to provision his gathering.","A smaller hand has written beneath the seal: They have ordered us not to give him bread. They have not ordered the officers to return ours."]},
 {id:'dawn-bloom-writ',room:'philomere',character:null,title:'A Dawn Bloom denunciation',stage:2,at:[39,20],text:["The civic council condemns Darius as a heretic who denies the judgement of God and attacks the lawful levy. His rejection of the enemy’s altar does not excuse his rejection of ours. Report the unmarked supply carts.","Someone has pinned a First Light order beside it. The seals differ. The same name is condemned on both pages."]},
 {id:'dorelem-rumour',room:'dorelem',character:'REV-ALICE',title:'The cream cloth knot',stage:1,at:[19,20],text:["See this knot? No crest, no blessing stitched into it. It means the cart carries food for whoever is hungry. A woman from your side tied mine. A man from theirs hauled the wheel out of the ditch. Both commands have posted Darius as a heretic now. Neither noticed whose hands repaired the cart.","The watch calls us thieves. We took two army wagons and returned the grain to the farms named in their ledgers. They left that part off the notice. Darius says a banner is a fine place to hide somebody else’s supper."]},
 {id:'north-road-cart',room:'northRoad2',character:'REV-ALICE',title:'The unmarked relief cart',stage:2,at:[24,22],text:["Your column burned a Dawn Bloom store. The Dawn Bloom patrol burned the farm that had supplied it. We spent the night pulling children out of the same ditch. Tell me which army fed them.","Darius keeps our western carters and eastern ditch crews at the same table. It is harder than making a speech. They have buried people killed by one another’s sons."],aid:true,battle:'TRN-REV-ALICE'},
 {id:'hospice-common-men',room:'philomere',character:'REV-MARYAM',title:'Names without banners',stage:2,at:[29,23],text:["Hugh’s brother died carrying your army’s flour. Mine died guarding a Dawn Bloom sluice. We argue over whose officer lied first. Then we change the dressings together.","I still pray, though Darius no longer does. He has never asked me to stop. We have cut supply ropes on both roads. An army that cannot march cannot burn another village. But last week a cart of medicine was delayed with the ammunition. Good intentions did not cool those fevers."],reaction:'medicine'},
 {id:'summit-search',room:'passSummit',character:null,title:'A survey with no army seal',stage:2,clue:'summit',at:[23,18],text:["A folded survey records Caelynx’s cairns, the hour of its appearance and the safest approach to Watcher’s Rest. Grain deliveries are pencilled into the margin in the same hand.","A note at the bottom reads: No soldiers at the shelf. Ask what it protects before deciding what it could protect for us. The page bears a plain cream thread instead of a military seal."]},
 {id:'fossil-visitor',room:'passCrystalPocket',character:'REV-MARYAM',title:'What the visitor asked',stage:2,at:[29,25],text:["A man came before you. Not an officer. He wanted to know whether a creature buried before either kingdom existed could still recognise an order.","Safiyya told him fossils were lives, not engines. He stayed to help label every sample before he left. That is what troubled her: he listened, and still asked for a map of the high shelf."]},
 {id:'cybressa-levy',room:'cybressa',character:'REV-FARID',title:'The recruit who went home',stage:3,at:[29,19],text:["I wore Dawn Bloom colours until they requisitioned my mother’s last mule. Your men offered me a place if I changed my coat. I asked whether changing coats would bring the mule back.","Darius gave me a place without asking which prayer I knew. Your chaplains call him a heretic. Ours use the same word. It is the first thing their officers have agreed on in years. Do not mistake that for friendship with you, {player}. Every fort you take needs feeding. Somebody like my mother pays for that."],reaction:'grain',battle:'TRN-REV-FARID'},
 {id:'backwater-speaker',room:'philomereBackwaters',character:'REV-DARIUS',title:'Darius at the common table',stage:3,at:[26,20],text:["Hugh, give Maryam the dry stool. She has been standing in water since dawn. Farid, put the knife away; if we cannot share bread across this table, we have no business speaking for a valley.","I do not want your king’s place, {player}, or the place of the people he is fighting. I want a field that is still a field after the armies pass. Spare a ruler or kill one: the levy cart comes back wearing a different crest.","They call me a heretic from both pulpits. I once knelt at one of them. I watched both armies bless the hands that set our villages alight. I no longer believe there is a god to answer us. No true god could permit both sides to make such suffering holy."]},
 {id:'fort-search',room:'waranCave4',character:'REV-FARID',title:'The flooded survey',stage:3,clue:'fort',at:[24,18],text:["Our scouts were here for Waranwei, not the fort’s silver. Darius asked for every account of what it hunted and every place a hunter might find those creatures. He wanted to know what kind of force could make a siege army afraid to advance.","I thought he wanted a weapon for the revolt. Then I found the rest of the list: tidal changes, how far a storm could reach, how long a town would have after a warning bell. No plans to conquer a place. Plans for what might make it too frightened to fight."]},
 {id:'court-search',room:'courtApproach',character:'REV-ALICE',title:'A court without an owner',stage:3,clue:'court',at:[23,25],text:["Darius asked whether the old court belonged to its king, or merely chose to follow him. I told him a dead king could not answer. He said living kings had proved no better at it.","There were thirteen places marked on his map. Beside each, the flood line of a town or the reach of a fire. He wants both armies to fear what they keep doing to our homes. I asked whose homes would stand inside his warning."]},
 {id:'wildfire-relief',room:'orchardFirebreak',character:'REV-MARYAM',title:'Who fights the fire',stage:4,at:[21,18],text:["The fire crossed a First Light ditch and a Dawn Bloom orchard. It did not stop to inspect either flag. Our crews cut this break with deserters from both patrols.","Your battles make names for fighters, {player}. Mine spends all day reading the names sewn into children’s coats so we can find their families."],reaction:'fates'},
 {id:'shrine-record',room:'sunkenShrine3',character:null,title:'The barrier proposal',stage:4,clue:'shrine',at:[28,22],text:["A water-stained proposal describes an old guardian turning armed vessels away while letting fishing boats pass. Darius has underlined the distinction three times.","In the margin: A warning means nothing unless both rulers believe it can be carried out. Let them fear for their own cities as our families fear for their fields. Beneath that, in another hand: Whose family pays for the first proof?"]},
 {id:'cossenne-darius',room:'cossenne',character:'REV-DARIUS',title:'A power neither banner owns',stage:4,at:[28,20],text:["They have offered me titles now. One army offered a tax exemption for my own village. The other offered to recognise our council if we recognised its war. They both thought the price of leaving everybody else hungry was a full table for me.","I do not believe either altar speaks for a god. A creature that can bring down a wall is not divine because frightened people kneel before it. Bring me what you have learned at the old sanctuaries. I will tell you why I sent people there."],reveal:true,battle:'TRN-REV-DARIUS'}
];
export const REVOLT_REVELATION=[
 "Yes. I sent scouts for Caelynx, for Waranwei’s hunting grounds, for the scattered court. I am trying to capture legendary and mythic Abyssals strong enough to make another war unthinkable. Neither army must believe it can strike without an answer.",
 "I have watched rulers call a hundred burned farms a necessary cost. They keep ordering wars because other people bear that cost. Let them know that another assault could bring devastation to their own gates. Let the possibility of losing everything weigh on their orders for once.",
 "I want an armistice that survives the first broken promise. If both sides know I can answer an invasion with a disaster neither can endure, fear will keep them still when honour and pity do not. They will have to live under the same dread they have given us. I no longer wait for divine judgement to teach them what pain means.",
 "Maryam asks whose children would suffer if I ever carried out the threat. Farid asks whether peace held at the edge of catastrophe is peace at all. I hear them. But I have buried enough people while waiting for powerful men to discover a conscience."
];
